= Solution
{parent=a}

Squaring the <modulus> and doubling the <complex argument> gives
$$
(2e^{i\pi/4})^2=4e^{i\pi/2}=\boxed{0+4i}.
$$
This uses <Euler's formula> $e^{i\theta}=\cos\theta+i\sin\theta$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Multiply numerator and denominator by the <complex conjugate> of the denominator:
$$
\frac{1+i}{1-i}=\frac{(1+i)^2}{(1-i)(1+i)}=\frac{2i}{2}=\boxed{0+i}.
$$
The denominator is the squared <modulus> $|1-i|^2=2$.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <complex number> $\sqrt3+i$ has <modulus> two and <principal argument> $\pi/6$. Therefore its <principal complex logarithm> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Log}(\sqrt3+i)=\ln2+i\frac\pi6.}
$$
If the source's logarithm means the full multivalued <complex logarithm>, rather than its principal value, the complete answer is $\ln2+i(\pi/6+2\pi n)$ with $n\in\mathbb Z$. A branch convention is necessary to obtain a unique imaginary part.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Use the <principal complex logarithm> at each stage of the nested <complex exponentiation>. Since $\operatorname{Log}i=i\pi/2$,
$$
i^i=e^{i\operatorname{Log}i}=e^{-\pi/2}.
$$
This inner value is positive real, so its <principal complex logarithm> is $-\pi/2$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{(i^i)^i=e^{i(-\pi/2)}=-i=0-i.}
$$
One must retain the parentheses: <complex exponentiation is not associative>. If all <complex logarithm> branches are allowed, the inner values are $e^{-\pi/2-2\pi k}$. Taking the outer logarithm with an independent integer branch index $\ell$ yields $\exp[-i\pi/2-2\pi ik-2\pi\ell]=-ie^{-2\pi\ell}$. Thus the complete multivalued set is $\{-ie^{-2\pi\ell}:\ell\in\mathbb Z\}$, and the boxed answer selects its principal evaluation.

= 2
{parent=2019}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The <determinant> is $z\cdot z^3-(-i)(-i)=z^4+1$. Thus $z^4=-1=e^{i(\pi+2\pi n)}$, and its four <polynomial roots> are
$$
\boxed{z_k=e^{i(\pi/4+k\pi/2)},\qquad k=0,1,2,3.}
$$
In Cartesian form these are $(1+i)/\sqrt2$, $(-1+i)/\sqrt2$, $(-1-i)/\sqrt2$ and $(1-i)/\sqrt2$. Their <moduli> are one and their <complex arguments> are equally spaced by $\pi/2$. The <Argand diagram> is therefore a square centered at the origin, with sides parallel to the coordinate axes.

\Image[2019-quartic-root-square.png]
{title=The four roots of z to the fourth equals minus one, plotted on the unit circle in the complex plane.}

= 3
{parent=2019}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The right-hand side depends on $x+y$, suggesting the substitution $u=x+y$. Then $y'=u'-1$, so the <ordinary differential equation> becomes
$$
u'=2-\frac4u=\frac{2(u-2)}u.
$$
The original equation is undefined at $u=0$. If $u\ne2$, this is a <separable differential equation>:
$$
\frac{u}{u-2}\,du=2\,dx,\qquad
u+2\ln|u-2|=2x+C.
$$
Substituting back gives the implicit family
$$
\boxed{y-x+2\ln|x+y-2|=C,\qquad x+y\ne0,2.}
$$
Differentiating the implicit equation gives $y'-1+2(1+y')/(x+y-2)=0$, or $(x+y)y'=x+y-4$, verifying it on its permitted intervals.

Division by $u-2$ excluded the constant solution $u=2$, which must be restored separately:
$$
\boxed{y=2-x.}
$$
It satisfies $y'=-1=(2-4)/2$. Together these give all local real solutions on intervals where $x+y\ne0$.

= 4
{parent=2019}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

On the interval $x>0$ containing the initial point, divide the <linear ordinary differential equation> by $x$ to obtain $y'-2y/x=x$. Its <integrating factor> is $e^{\int-2\,dx/x}=x^{-2}$, giving
$$
\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{y}{x^2}\right)=\frac1x,
\qquad y=x^2(\ln x+C).
$$
The initial condition gives $1=C$. Hence
$$
\boxed{y(x)=x^2(1+\ln x),\qquad x>0.}
$$
Direct differentiation gives $y'=x(3+2\ln x)$, so $xy'-2y=x^2$ and $y(1)=1$. The coefficient of $y'$ vanishes at zero; the boxed expression is the solution on the regular initial-value interval, rather than an assertion of unique continuation through zero.

= 5
{parent=2019}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

A <normal vector> to the <plane> is $n=(3,4,12)^T$, with <Euclidean norm> $\|n\|=\sqrt{9+16+144}=13$. For $p=(1,1,1)^T$, $n\cdot p=19$, so the <distance from a point to a plane> is
$$
\boxed{d=\frac{|n\cdot p-26|}{\|n\|}=\frac7{13}.}
$$
To verify that this is the minimum, the displacement to the <closest point on a plane> is parallel to $n$. Put $q=p+tn$ and require $n\cdot q=26$. This gives $t=7/169$ and
$$
q=\frac1{169}(190,197,253)^T.
$$
For every other point $r$ on the <plane>, the displacement $r-q$ is orthogonal to $q-p$. The <Pythagorean theorem> gives $\|r-p\|^2=\|q-p\|^2+\|r-q\|^2\geq(7/13)^2$, proving minimality.

= 6
{parent=2019}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Let $Av=\lambda v$ with $v\ne0$, using the standard complex <Hermitian inner product>. Then
$$
v^\dagger Av=\lambda v^\dagger v.
$$
Taking the <complex conjugate> and using $A^\dagger=-A$ yields $\overline\lambda v^\dagger v=v^\dagger A^\dagger v=-\lambda v^\dagger v$. Since $v^\dagger v>0$,
$$
\boxed{\overline\lambda=-\lambda,\qquad \operatorname{Re}\lambda=0.}
$$
Thus every <eigenvalue> of an <anti-Hermitian matrix> lies on the imaginary axis, including the possible zero <eigenvalue>; each nonzero <eigenvalue> is purely imaginary.

Now take $Au=\lambda u$ and $Av=\mu v$. On one hand $u^\dagger Av=\mu u^\dagger v$. On the other hand
$$
u^\dagger Av=-(Au)^\dagger v=-\overline\lambda u^\dagger v=\lambda u^\dagger v.
$$
Consequently $(\mu-\lambda)u^\dagger v=0$. Distinct <eigenvalues> therefore imply
$$
\boxed{u^\dagger v=0,}
$$
which is the required orthogonality of the <eigenvectors>.

= 7
{parent=2019}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

Write the given <matrix> as $A$. Expanding its <characteristic polynomial> along the middle column gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\det(\lambda I-A)
&=(\lambda+2)\big[(\lambda-7)(\lambda+8)+54\big]\\
&=(\lambda+2)(\lambda^2+\lambda-2)
=(\lambda-1)(\lambda+2)^2.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the <eigenvalues> are $1,-2,-2$, counted with <algebraic multiplicity>.

For $\lambda=1$, solving $(A-I)v=0$ gives $z=2x$ and $y=-x$. For $\lambda=-2$, solving $(A+2I)v=0$ gives $z=3x$, with $y$ free. A convenient <eigenbasis> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c}
\lambda&\text{eigenvector}\\\hline
1&(1,-1,2)^T\\
-2&(1,0,3)^T\\
-2&(0,1,0)^T
\end{array}}
$$
The last two <eigenvectors> span the two-dimensional <eigenspace> for $-2$. The three columns have <determinant> $-1$, so they are linearly independent and form a <basis> of $\mathbb R^3$. In particular the repeated <eigenvalue> causes no failure of <diagonalizability> here.

= 8
{parent=2019}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

Apply the <vector triple product identity> $p\times(c\times d)=c(p\cdot d)-d(p\cdot c)$ with $p=a\times b$. This gives
$$
(a\times b)\times(c\times d)
=c\big((a\times b)\cdot d\big)-d\big((a\times b)\cdot c\big).
$$
For column <vectors> $a,b,d$, the <scalar triple product> is $(a\times b)\cdot d=\det(a,b,d)$; replacing $d$ by $c$ gives the other coefficient. Therefore
$$
\boxed{(a\times b)\times(c\times d)=\det(a,b,d)c-\det(a,b,c)d.}
$$
The signs follow the stated column convention and the cyclic invariance of the <scalar triple product>. No independence assumptions on the four <vectors> are needed.

